@page "/null-policy"
@inject JobRepository Jobs

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] public HttpContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private sealed record Panel(string Name, string Detail, string Code, IReadOnlyList<PanelRow> Rows);

    private sealed record PanelRow(long JobId, string? CustomerName, string? Notes, string? DisplayLabel, decimal? Amount);

    private List<Panel> panels = [];

    private static readonly Job Template = new()
    {
        CustomerName = "unknown",
        Notes = "template",
        DisplayLabel = "template",
        Amount = 0.00m
    };

    private static readonly PlainJob PlainTemplate = new()
    {
        CustomerName = "unknown",
        Notes = "template",
        DisplayLabel = "template",
        Amount = 0.00m
    };

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var cancellationToken = Context.RequestAborted;

        var keep = new MapOptions { NullHandling = NullHandling.Keep, MatchNormalizedNames = true };

        panels =
        [
            new Panel(
                "EmptyString (default)",
                "A non-nullable string is guaranteed non-null. The template here is a bare POCO, so a nullable member has nothing to keep and stays null.",
                "reader.MapTo<Job>(options);   // options.NullHandling == NullHandling.EmptyString",
                await Rows(new Job(), MapOptions.Default, cancellationToken)),

            new Panel(
                "Keep",
                "Nothing is written when the column is DBNull, so the template POCO survives, including the default it declares for Amount. Notes is the exception: the model puts NullAs(Null) on that member, and a member attribute wins over the option, so notes is forced to null here.",
                "reader.MapTo(template, new MapOptions { NullHandling = NullHandling.Keep });",
                await Rows(Template, keep, cancellationToken)),

            new Panel(
                "Keep, without the attribute on notes",
                "PlainJob is the same model with no NullAs on notes. Same option, same template, same rows: notes comes back as the template value. That is the whole difference between these two panels.",
                "reader.MapTo(plainTemplate, new MapOptions { NullHandling = NullHandling.Keep });",
                await PlainRows(PlainTemplate, keep, cancellationToken)),

            new Panel(
                "Null",
                "Every member the mapper can write is forced to null, so the defaults the template declares are overwritten.",
                "reader.MapTo(template, new MapOptions { NullHandling = NullHandling.Null });",
                await Rows(Template, new MapOptions { NullHandling = NullHandling.Null, MatchNormalizedNames = true }, cancellationToken))
        ];
    }

    private async Task<List<PanelRow>> Rows(Job template, MapOptions options, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var jobs = await Jobs.GetSampleAsync(template, options, cancellationToken);

        return jobs.Select(job => new PanelRow(job.JobId, job.CustomerName, job.Notes, job.DisplayLabel, job.Amount)).ToList();
    }

    private async Task<List<PanelRow>> PlainRows(PlainJob template, MapOptions options, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var jobs = await Jobs.GetPlainSampleAsync(template, options, cancellationToken);

        return jobs.Select(job => new PanelRow(job.JobId, job.CustomerName, job.Notes, job.DisplayLabel, job.Amount)).ToList();
    }

    private static string Show(string? value) => value is null ? "null" : (value == string.Empty ? "empty string" : value);
}

<PageTitle>Null policy</PageTitle>

<h2>Null policy</h2>

<p class="lede">
    The same seven rows, mapped four ways. Rows 10, 20 and 30 have no customer name and no notes;
    rows 7, 14, 21 and 42 have no amount. The template POCO declares defaults for those columns
    (<code>unknown</code>, <code>template</code>, <code>0.00m</code>), which is what each policy starts
    from. Nulls enter the code base at the data source, so that is where they are resolved.
</p>

@foreach (var panel in panels)
{
    <h3>@panel.Name</h3>

    <p class="note">@panel.Detail</p>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@panel.Code" />

    <table class="grid">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>job_id</th>
                <th>customer_name</th>
                <th>notes</th>
                <th>display_label</th>
                <th>amount</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var job in panel.Rows)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td><a href="/jobs/@job.JobId">@job.JobId</a></td>
                    <td>@Show(job.CustomerName)</td>
                    <td>@Show(job.Notes)</td>
                    <td>@Show(job.DisplayLabel)</td>
                    <td>@(job.Amount?.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) ?? "null")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
}

<h3>Why the nulls are still there in the Keep panel</h3>

<p class="note">
    Two things produce them, and both are the documented behaviour.
</p>

<p class="note">
    <strong>The member attribute wins.</strong> The policy is resolved as
    <code>member.Handling ?? options.NullHandling</code>, so a <code>NullAs</code> on the model takes
    precedence over whatever the options say. <code>Job.Notes</code> carries
    <code>NullAs(NullHandling.Null)</code>, so it is forced to null under every policy, including Keep.
    The third panel is the same run against a model without that attribute, which is why notes comes
    back as the template value there.
</p>

<p class="note">
    <strong>Keep preserves the template, it does not invent a value.</strong> The template POCO declares
    a default for <code>Amount</code> (<code>0.00m</code>), which is why the two Keep panels show
    <code>0.00</code> where the column is DBNull and the Null panel shows <code>null</code>: the first two
    keep what the template holds, the third overwrites it. If the template had nothing in it, Keep could
    only produce null, because there is no empty instance of a decimal.
</p>

<p class="note">
    The default policy is the one that lets the rest of the code base skip <code>!= null</code> checks:
    a member the compiler considers non-nullable is never null. That guarantee is per member, and a
    <code>NullAs(Null)</code> on the model is an explicit decision to opt that member out of it.
</p>
